Track 3: Dastkhat (Under the Chronometer)
- Story & Context: By the time the West Yorkshire underground circuit collapsed under police crackdowns in 2008, sound engineer Julian Finch had retreated to the flooded gritstone quarry beneath Stoodley Pike to complete what locals still refer to as the Submerged Horologue. Dragging an octagonal, forty-pound pallasite meteorite through the bracken, he fused it to the salvaged balance wheel of an 18th-century parish clock and sank the entire contraption thirty feet into the freezing, sulfurous quarry pool. Hydrophone cables snaking up to a pirate FM transmitter broadcast the muffled clank of extraterrestrial iron against brass gears, accompanied only by a ghost-loop of Parveen's tabla bols floating over the fog to guide stranded trespassers home. As the transmitter battery finally froze to death in the sleet, the band's departing chant faded into the moors: "Count the centuries in the copper skin, there is no name left to sign."
